### Round 1: Weird facts that aren't true facts (10)

1. **What's the only letter of the alphabet that doesn't appear in any US state name?** *Q.*
2. **What flavor of jelly bean was the result of a factory mix-up that turned into a Jelly Belly flagship?** *Buttered popcorn.*
3. **What was Coca-Cola's original color before food dye?** *Green.*
4. **What's the collective noun for a group of flamingos?** *A flamboyance.*
5. **What's the only food that never spoils?** *Honey.*
6. **What body part is the same size from age 5 to age 50?** *Your eyeballs.*
7. **What was the first item ever bought on the internet, in 1994?** *A Sting CD (Ten Summoner's Tales).*
8. **In which country is it illegal to keep a single pet goldfish alone in a bowl?** *Switzerland (goldfish are considered social animals).*
9. **What's the term for the dot above the lowercase letter "i" and "j"?** *A tittle.*
10. **Which is older: the Eiffel Tower or sliced bread?** *The Eiffel Tower (1889 vs sliced bread's first commercial sale in 1928).*

### Round 2: Animals doing animal things (10)

11. **Which animal has three hearts and blue blood?** *The octopus.*
12. **A group of owls is called what?** *A parliament.*
13. **Which bird can fly backward?** *The hummingbird.*
14. **What mammal lays eggs?** *The platypus (the echidna also qualifies).*
15. **Which animal sleeps the most each day?** *The koala (20 to 22 hours).*
16. **What's a baby porcupine called?** *A porcupette.*
17. **Which animal's fingerprints are so similar to a human's they've reportedly confused crime scene investigators?** *The koala.*
18. **What's the loudest animal on earth, relative to size?** *The pistol shrimp. Its claw snap can stun fish.*
19. **Cows have best friends. True or false?** *True. Stress markers spike when they're separated.*
20. **What animal can be detected from space by its methane emissions?** *Cows.*

### Round 3: Geography surprises (10)

21. **Which two countries share the longest land border on Earth?** *Canada and the United States (5,525 miles).*
22. **What's the only sea on Earth with no coastline?** *The Sargasso Sea.*
23. **What's the highest capital city in the world by elevation?** *La Paz, Bolivia.*
24. **How many time zones does Russia span?** *Eleven.*
25. **Which continent has no native snakes?** *Antarctica.*
26. **The Pacific Ocean is wider than the Moon's diameter at its widest point. True or false?** *True.*
27. **Which US state has the only one-syllable name?** *Maine.*
28. **Which African country was never colonised by European powers (excluding short occupations)?** *Ethiopia.*
29. **What's the largest desert on Earth?** *Antarctica (technically a polar desert, larger than the Sahara).*
30. **Which country has the most lakes in the world?** *Canada (over 60% of the world's lakes).*

### Round 4: Movies and TV (10)

31. **What pantry item was used as the blood in the "Psycho" shower scene?** *Chocolate syrup.*
32. **What was the first feature-length animated film?** *Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs (1937).*
33. **In "The Office" (US), what's the name of Dwight's beet farm?** *Schrute Farms.*
34. **Which director cameos in nearly every one of his own films?** *Alfred Hitchcock.*
35. **In "Friends," what is Ross Geller's middle name?** *Eustace.*
36. **What is Bruce Willis's character's first name in "Die Hard"?** *John (McClane).*
37. **What animal is the original mascot of the MGM logo?** *A lion.*
38. **Which film holds the record for most extras in a single shot (approx 300,000)?** *Gandhi (1982).*
39. **In "Toy Story," what's the name on the bottom of Woody's boot?** *Andy.*
40. **Which TV character's full name is "Sheldon Lee Cooper, PhD, ScD"?** *Sheldon from The Big Bang Theory.*

### Round 5: Music (10)

41. **Which Beatles song has the most cover versions ever recorded?** *Yesterday.*
42. **What was the first music video ever played on MTV?** *"Video Killed the Radio Star" by The Buggles (August 1, 1981).*
43. **Whose real name is Stefani Joanne Angelina Germanotta?** *Lady Gaga.*
44. **Which band was originally called "The Quarrymen"?** *The Beatles.*
45. **Which artist holds the record for most Grammy wins (as of 2025)?** *Beyoncé (35+).*
46. **What instrument does Lisa Simpson play?** *The baritone saxophone.*
47. **What was the highest-grossing concert tour of all time?** *Taylor Swift's Eras Tour (over $2 billion).*
48. **Which singer rejected the role of Neo in "The Matrix"?** *Will Smith.* (Not a singer in that decade, but a household-name no.)
49. **In what year did Spotify launch in the US?** *2011.*
50. **Which musician was knighted while wearing a pirate costume in a sketch (in real life)?** *None — trick question. But Sir Paul McCartney has a knighthood and a pirate costume in his on-stage history.* Skip the gotcha — replace with: **What instrument has 47 strings on a standard concert version?** *The pedal harp.*

### Round 6: Sports oddities (10)

51. **Which sport was invented in Massachusetts in 1891 to combat winter boredom?** *Basketball.*
52. **In golf, what's the term for one stroke under par on a hole?** *A birdie.*
53. **What's the only Olympic event where men and women compete directly against each other?** *Equestrian.*
54. **What's the maximum break possible in snooker?** *147.*
55. **In which sport does "love" mean zero?** *Tennis.*
56. **Which boxer was nicknamed "The Greatest"?** *Muhammad Ali.*
57. **What is the only sport ever played on the Moon?** *Golf. Alan Shepard hit two balls in 1971.*
58. **What's a perfect score in ten-pin bowling?** *300.*
59. **How long is a regulation NBA shot clock?** *24 seconds.*
60. **What is the most-watched TV event in human history?** *The 2008 Beijing Olympics opening ceremony (estimated 4 billion viewers).*

### Round 7: Food and drink (10)

61. **What is the most stolen food in the world?** *Cheese.*
62. **In which Italian city was the Margherita pizza invented in 1889?** *Naples.*
63. **What spice is the most expensive by weight?** *Saffron.*
64. **What was the original color of carrots before the orange variety was bred?** *Purple (and white and yellow).*
65. **Why are bananas considered berries but strawberries aren't?** *Berries are botanically fruits from a single ovary with seeds inside; strawberries are aggregate accessory fruits with seeds on the outside.*
66. **What does the "Dr" in Dr Pepper stand for?** *Nothing official. Style choice by the inventor.*
67. **In Japan, Kit Kat is a popular exam-time gift because its name resembles which Japanese phrase?** *"Kitto Katsu" — "you will surely win."*
68. **What is the world's most consumed beverage after water?** *Tea.*
69. **What pepper variety topped the Scoville scale in 2023?** *Pepper X.*
70. **Which fast-food chain has the most physical locations worldwide (as of 2025)?** *Subway.*

### Round 8: History (10)

71. **How many years did the Hundred Years' War actually last?** *116.*
72. **In what year did the Berlin Wall fall?** *1989.*
73. **Who was the first person to step on the Moon?** *Neil Armstrong (1969).*
74. **What was Cleopatra's actual heritage?** *Macedonian Greek (Ptolemaic dynasty), not ethnically Egyptian.*
75. **Which empire was the largest contiguous land empire in history?** *The Mongol Empire.*
76. **In what year was Pluto demoted from planet status?** *2006.*
77. **Who invented the World Wide Web (and gave it away)?** *Tim Berners-Lee (1989 at CERN).*
78. **What was the name of the ship the Pilgrims sailed to America on?** *The Mayflower.*
79. **Which two famous Americans died on the same day, July 4, 1826, exactly 50 years after the Declaration of Independence?** *Thomas Jefferson and John Adams.*
80. **What's the official cause of the 2000 hanging-chad election ruling?** *Bush v. Gore (Supreme Court halted the Florida recount).*

### Round 9: Words and language (10)

81. **What's the longest English word that uses each vowel exactly once, in order?** *Facetiously.*
82. **What is the fear of long words ironically called?** *Hippopotomonstrosesquippedaliophobia.*
83. **What's the only common English word that ends in -mt?** *Dreamt.*
84. **How many countries have English as an official language?** *Around 67.*
85. **What's the most-used letter in written English?** *E.*
86. **What does "OK" originally stand for?** *"Oll Korrect" (a deliberately mis-spelled "all correct" from 1839 Boston slang).*
87. **What did the word "nice" originally mean in Middle English?** *Foolish or silly.*
